WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.049
Axing a fan favorite character is a great
way for writers to raise dramatic stakes,

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.960
and audiences will generally let them get
away with it.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.500
But sometimes a character will be headed for
the chopping block... only to get a last-minute

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.000
reprieve.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:18.179
These are the times when writers almost broke
our hearts, but then chose not to and fan

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.040
favorites who were originally supposed to
die got to live on.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:22.980
Daryl Dixon

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.720
Walking Dead creator Frank Darabont wrote
the part of Daryl Dixon especially for Norman

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.920
Reedus the character never appears in the
comics the show is based on.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:36.380
Daryl was originally supposed to make a few
appearances, and then exit the way most Walking

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.640
Dead guests do as zombie food.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:44.020
But a funny thing happened: fans loved the
character, even though he was initially totally

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:44.520
unlikable.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.020
Darabont gave Reedus a chance to develop the
role into something special, and over many

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:54.750
seasons the character has become so popular
that Robert Kirkman, creator of the still-ongoing

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.540
comic series, has implied he'll eventually
write Daryl into the comics as well.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.220
"What, no pictures?"

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.100
Jesse Pinkman

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.000
Breaking Bad began with a likable protagonist
and, over the course of five seasons, turned

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.430
him into a strangely likeable villain.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.740
While the show was mostly focused on Walter
White, it also dealt with moral situations

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.540
faced by Walter's family, and especially young
partner in crime Jesse Pinkman, played by

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.440
Aaron Paul.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:27.160
Originally, Jesse was supposed to meet the
reaper at the end of season one, thereby accelerating

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.420
Walter White's transformation into a ruthless
drug lord.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.340
Legend has it that the 2007-2008 writers'
strike, which shortened Breaking Bad's first

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.720
season and forced plot changes, saved Jesse.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.970
But the strike really just gave creator Vince
Gilligan time to reconsider.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.560
Aaron Paul was just too great to let go.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.600
"It became pretty clear early on that it would
be a huge, colossal mistake to kill off Jesse."

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.100
Jack Shephard

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:58.900
Perhaps nobody benefited more from the narrative
aimlessness of Lost than its star, Matthew

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.780
Fox, who played primary protagonist Jack Shephard.

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:06.520
Executive producer J.J. Abrams originally
planned to introduce the clear, obvious lead

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.100
in the first episode only to sacrifice him
by episode's end.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.310
Abrams thought this would keep audiences on
their toes, and even cast Michael Keaton in

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:18.040
the role, who was thrilled with the idea of
getting a network TV role without having to

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.580
commit to the entire series.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:23.780
Keaton eventually backed out when showrunners
decided that the character would be kept alive.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.880
As Keaton explained to Access Hollywood,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.470
"An hour show every week…

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.480
I've got stuff to do, I've got a life to lead.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.760
When would I fish?"

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.460
Han Solo

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.040
Harrison Ford has long seemed ambivalent toward
his most famous role, Star Wars' iconic Han

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:43.540
Solo.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.620
It seems like a minor miracle that Solo survived
as long as he did, with Ford ruthlessly pushing

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.440
for his own character's demise during the
original series.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.940
Solo was left ambiguously frozen at the end
of The Empire Strikes Back specifically because

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.480
nobody knew whether Ford would come back for
a third installment.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:07.620
When he was coaxed to return, he lobbied George
Lucas unsuccessfully for Solo's "noble sacrifice"

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.880
in Return of the Jedi.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:14.800
"George didn't think there was any future
in dead Han toys"

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:21.060
Of course, spoiler alert, the character finally
did meet his end in The Force Awakens and

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.470
if any of the many fan theories are correct,
it may have been just the noble sacrifice

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.200
that Ford always wanted.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.300
Ellen Ripley

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:36.720
1979's Alien was one of the first genre films
to feature a strong female lead, but in production,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.420
Sigourney Weaver's Ellen Ripley wasn't necessarily
central to the film.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:45.410
Speaking with Entertainment Weekly, director
Ridley Scott revealed his brutal alternate

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.520
ending for Alien:

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:53.900
"The alien holds onto the door she harpoons
it it makes no difference and it slams through

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.120
her mask and rips her head off."

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:59.840
Then, the creature would have called the ground
crew, saying, "I'm signing off" in the captain's

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:00.340
voice.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.800
Fortunately, Scott thought better of this
silly idea, allowing Ripley to live on to

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.490
become an even more badass character in an
even better film.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:15.660
"This is Ripley last survivor of the Nostromo…
signing off"

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:17.240
Nebula

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:23.200
In Guardians of the Galaxy Volume 2, Nebula,
played by Karen Gillan, revealed herself to

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.080
be a more complex character than the jealousy-driven
villain she first appeared to be.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.651
But she almost didn't get to stay in the MCU,
having been written out at the end of the

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:33.900
first Guardians film.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.060
Speaking with ScreenCrush, Gillan said Nebula
was saved by rewrites.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.340
She revealed that her final scene was never
actually shot, saying,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.980
"We never got to that point.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.900
As soon as we would get near to it there would
be a new script."

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:50.650
Gillan also appeared in Avengers: Infinity
War and will definitely be in its untitled

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:55.960
sequel, for which she can thank Guardians
writer/director James Gunn and his indecisiveness.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.860
"Anyways, happy trails"

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:03.600
Mr. Spock

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:10.140
Leonard Nimoy had a famously complicated relationship
with his most famous character, Mr. Spock.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:15.180
He told the producers of 1979's Star Trek:
The Motion Picture that he was only going

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.820
to play Spock one last time, so the character
wasn't even included in the first draft of

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:22.240
the screenplay for Star Trek II: The Wrath
of Khan.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:27.460
But then, screenwriter Jack B. Sowards had
an idea: why not give Spock an epic final

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:27.980
scene?

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:32.500
"Live long and prosper"

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.400
Nimoy liked the idea, but subsequent rewrites
pushed Spock's final scene farther and farther

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.340
back, until it was at the end of the movie.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:50.460
Nimoy approved each draft, and loved Sowards'
writing so much that it made him want to keep

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:54.220
playing Spock which he did, for another 27
years.

